From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: athexweb3 <athexweb3@nirapod.org>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 20:08:32 +0600
Subject: [PATCH] Do not assume that system calls preserve the vector registers

A system call is assumed to preserve every callee-saved register in
full (eval_syscall_preserves, and syscall_kill in register allocation).
That's too strong for the vector registers. With -call-conv windows,
xmm6-xmm15 are callee-saved, but a Jasmin xreg is the 256-bit ymm, and
the Microsoft x64 ABI makes the upper half of ymm0-ymm15 volatile. So a
u256 live across #randombytes can sit in ymm6 and lose its upper 128
bits. AAPCS64 has the same gap for v8-v15, where only the low 64 bits
are preserved, which matters once armv8a has vector registers.

A system call is now assumed to preserve only the callee-saved
registers that aren't vector registers (syscall_preserved). The model
and syscall_kill both use it, and on the OCaml side the kill set calls
the extracted definition. callee_saved doesn't change, so export
functions still save and restore the vector registers in full.

It's conservative: a register is either preserved whole or not at all,
so a u128 in xmm6, which the ABI does keep, isn't assumed to survive
either. Only syscall programs compiled with -call-conv windows change.
An export that issues a syscall, directly or not, now saves and
restores ymm6-ymm15, and a vector value live across a syscall has to be
spilled.

Co-authored-by: orvenweb3 <orvenweb3@gmail.com>
